[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one bug, one fix queued. Its reports were merged on
subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), so nobody read the bodies.
Read for what they show, they are three problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser does not close #4471. The other two problems have their own causes and need their own fixes.
</Callout>

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** All our tenants on file write the
day first, so their dates come out with day and month swapped ("7 April went out on 4 July").
This is the bug #4471 is named after, and the fix that is queued.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type the month first.** Its date field shows
`MM/DD/YYYY`, then reads the value the tenant's way, day first. People follow the hint, so the
date flips there before the parser runs. It shows up only in reports that came in through the form
at tenants on booking-v3. The body reads the same as a parser report, so subject and body alone
cannot tell them apart; tenant and channel can. A parser fix will not touch it: the hint must
match the locale, or the form must stop guessing.

**`one-day`: the date slips by one day.** It is not flipped. A meeting on Tuesday 12 May shows as
Monday 11 May; a client in Lisbon got Tuesday and one in Toronto got Monday; an invite arrived
before the booking was made. That is a time-zone shift (a date stored or sent at midnight in one
zone and read in another), not a format bug. The desk read these as one-day moves and footnoted
them under `parser`; they belong in a ticket of their own. The counts above follow the desk's
view: these reports stay counted where they are filed, and the one-day ticket counts them again.

## How the reports were counted

- The export has overlapping pages, so some reports come twice. One id, one report.
- `parser` and `hint` come from `causeOf` in `causes.ts`: the tenant's locale and booking form,
  not intake's tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and it is wrong whenever someone
  in a day-first business reports from an `en-US` browser.
- `one-day` is the desk's own list (`desk-notes.json`). Like the desk's table, this page counts
  those reports under their cause as well. Every other report names two dates that are the same
  day and month swapped, or says the dates are mixed up.

## What to do with #4471

- Keep #4471 for `parser`. Open its two siblings and move their reports there.
- Do not mark #4471 known again until the counts on this page are split across three tickets.
- Dedupe on subject merged three bugs into one ticket. When an incident's reports describe
  different things in their bodies, split before you merge.

